# Mutation cases for the C++ half of a host-served Event I/O Processor.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/host_processor_dispatch_cpp.cases`.
#
# Sibling of `host_processor_dispatch.cases`, which studies the same contract
# on the Rust runtime. The two backends do not share this code — Rust keeps a
# `HostProcessorRegistry` in its own module, C++ keeps the map on
# `StaticExecutionEngine` — so the clause needs a case on each side. A mutation
# of the Rust registry says nothing about what the C++ engine does.
#
# The contract has two halves that fail in opposite directions:
#
#   * a registered handler must RECEIVE the send, with the payload the author
#     wrote, and its reply must arrive as an event;
#   * a declared type with NOTHING registered must raise `error.execution`,
#     because from the document's side an act nobody performed is one fact
#     however the wiring failed.
#
# A dispatch that silently succeeded into an empty registry would satisfy the
# first and destroy the second — it is the original defect wearing the new
# feature's clothes — so the cases below attack both.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^IntegrationTests$'
mutation_targets sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h tools/codegen/templates/actions/send.jinja2
# The file whose assertions produce every verdict below, measured rather than
# guessed. A ctest entry names a binary of twenty-odd sources; naming that
# whole list here would put a long round on half of all pushes.
mutation_oracles tests/integration/HostProcessorAotTest.cpp

# ── The handler stops being reached ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The dispatch finds the handler and then does not call it. Every counter the
# fixture keeps reads as it would for a handler that answered nothing, which is
# a legitimate outcome — so nothing but the recorded request tells the two
# apart, and that is exactly what `ARegisteredHandlerReceivesTheSendAndItsReply
# Arrives` asserts on.

mutation_case "the dispatch never calls the handler" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        auto response = it->second(request);""",
     """        auto response = std::optional<::SCE::HostSendResponse>();""")
PY

# ── A reply is accepted and dropped ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The handler runs, answers, and the answer never becomes an event. The send
# still counts as served — `performHostSend` returned a value, so the generated
# site raises no `error.execution` — and the document simply waits forever for
# a reply that was produced and discarded. This is the request/reply half of
# the feature, and losing it is invisible to any check that only asks whether
# the handler ran.

mutation_case "the handler's reply never becomes an event" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        if (response.has_value() && !response->eventName.empty()) {
            raiseExternal(response->eventName, response->eventData);
        }""",
     """        if (false && response.has_value() && !response->eventName.empty()) {
            raiseExternal(response->eventName, response->eventData);
        }""")
PY

# ── An empty registry reads as success ──────────────────────────────
#
# The other direction, and the one that matters most: a declared type with
# nothing registered must raise `error.execution`. Remove the emitted guard and
# the document proceeds as though its act had been carried out — the build
# promising delivery it never performed, which is the defect the whole feature
# was written to avoid rather than relocate.
#
# Mutating the TEMPLATE rather than the engine because the guard is emitted per
# send site. No `mutation_regen` is declared and none is needed: the ctest
# runner's `cmake --build` runs the generation step, so a template edit reaches
# the binary the round measures — which is why `mutation_check_template_bridge`
# exempts this runner rather than this casefile.

mutation_case "an unregistered processor is treated as served" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/actions/send.jinja2",
     """    if (!hostServed.has_value() && !engine.hasEventProcessor("{{ action.send_type | escape_cpp }}")) {""",
     """    if (false && !hostServed.has_value() && !engine.hasEventProcessor("{{ action.send_type | escape_cpp }}")) {""")
PY

# ── The registry stops being keyed ──────────────────────────────────
#
# A lookup that served any registered handler would deliver a document's acts
# to a processor it never named. §scxml-6.2.5 makes the identifier the whole
# address, so a registry that ignores it is not a registry.

mutation_case "any registered handler serves any type" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        const auto it = hostProcessors_.find(request.processorType);""",
     """        const auto it = hostProcessors_.begin();""")
PY
